From e854f7040f71952035974a2d431f9e3285a779d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 10:16:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add Static Site block type (lime, folder icon)

Fixes up and completes the Static Site card you'd already added to the
new-block modal (it was a copy-pasted Load Balancer stub -- wrong link,
wrong icon, wrong description) and wires the type through end to end.

Fields:
- File path (required) -- the root directory to serve.
- Encode -- a multiselect of gzip/zstd/br. Caddy negotiates the actual
  format used per-request against the client's Accept-Encoding, so
  selecting more than one just widens what the server is willing to use.
- file_server is always on for this type (a static site block with it
  off wouldn't serve anything -- that's what the Custom block type is
  for), so there's no checkbox for it.
- Advanced options (collapsed by default, alongside the usual Create
  disabled): Hide (space-separated glob patterns, e.g. .git .env),
  Index files (space-separated, optional -- Caddy's own default applies
  if left blank), and a Directory browsing checkbox.

Rendering: file_server is a bare line when none of hide/index/browse are
set, or a `file_server { ... }` block when any of them are, e.g.:

    example.com {
        root * /var/www/html
        encode gzip br
        file_server {
            hide .git .env
            index index.html
            browse
        }
    }

Implementation:
- colors.py: static_site -> lime, the single place this is defined.
- caddyfile.py: render_static_site(site_header, path, encodings, browse,
  index, hide) builds the block as above. parse_conf_content() also
  learns to recognize this shape (a `root` directive, optionally an
  `encode` line, optionally a `file_server { ... }` block) in hand-edited
  or externally-created .conf files, extracting the same fields, so
  static site blocks dropped in outside the app are detected -- including
  their encode formats and file_server subdirectives -- the same as every
  other type.
- blocks.py: build_block_from_form() gains a static_site branch (path
  required; encode read via form.getlist() and filtered against the
  known formats so an unexpected value can never end up in a rendered
  .conf; hide/index/browse are plain fields); list_blocks() sorts by path
  and carries path/encode/browse/index/hide through to the site blocks
  table.
- views.py: static_site added to new_block()'s allowed block types.
- templates: block_form.html gets the File path field, the encode
  multiselect, and Hide/Index/Browse inside Advanced options (alongside
  Create disabled, new-block only); site_blocks.html gets a filter
  option, a type badge, and an upstream/target cell showing the path
  (+ the selected encode formats); preview.html gets a matching type
  badge and a details entry for path/encode/hide/index/browse. The
  folder icon (Tabler's icon-tabler-folder) is used consistently
  everywhere the type appears.

Verified: created static site blocks through the form (both a bare-minimum
one and one using every field) and confirmed the rendered .conf content is
correct in both cases; confirmed blocks list, edit (fields prefill
correctly, including the multiselect and checkbox), preview (all fields
shown), toggle, and delete correctly; confirmed both a simple and a fully
loaded externally-created static-site .conf (with a file_server { ... }
block) are correctly detected via the regex fallback parser, including
extracting encode formats and hide/index/browse from inside the block;
confirmed a spoofed/invalid encode form value is silently filtered rather
than ending up in the rendered Caddyfile; confirmed the missing-path
validation error renders without writing a file. Ran the full
create/edit/preview/toggle/delete regression across all five block types,
and pyflakes is clean.
